=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>S=
tunned, baffled and increasingly angry, the dismissed Iraqi soldiers gather=
ed every morning at the gates of the Green Zone. Some had a message for Was=
hington.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#3=
33;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CTell your president that we had to fight=
 for Saddam,=E2=80=9D one told me. =E2=80=9CWe are glad he=E2=80=99s gone.=
=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Another said he was 51 years old and could not m=
ake ends meet without his pension. =E2=80=9CI have five children,=E2=80=9D =
he said. =E2=80=9CMy son lost his leg. I work as a janitor. I am too old to=
 find a new job.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georg=
ia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>One man wearing a long dishdasha=
 robe =E2=80=94 I can still see his face in my mind=E2=80=99s eye =E2=80=94=
 asked the interpreter where I was from. When he heard =E2=80=9CU.S.,=E2=80=
=9D his face twisted in disgust and he spat a stream of liquid at me. Some =
landed on my shoe. I stared at it, unsure whether to wipe it off.</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span>I wondered if no one saw the widening gap between Iraqi hopes for a=
 post-Hussein future and what the U.S. was actually offering.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>Many Iraqis had unrealistic expectations. They wanted America to transf=
orm their country overnight into a Middle Eastern version of the shiny, ide=
alized American world they knew from the bootlegged movies they had watched=
 secretly under Hussein.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But what many more were unprepare=
d for was America=E2=80=99s disregard for the economic realities of their l=
ives. They were unprepared for America=E2=80=99s failure to realize that wh=
en you take away a person=E2=80=99s job, you take away their place in their=
 communities, their families, their dignity.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Only much lat=
er did I connect the fury of the man who spat on me with his feeling of hav=
ing been disrespected. It was just a few weeks after the fall of Saddam; a =
dangerous dynamic had already begun.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
